1
00:00:37,280 --> 00:00:41,640
CONVERSATION 1
Thank you for meeting with me, Stephen, at such short notice.

2
00:00:41,640 --> 00:00:43,380
Not a problem, Margaret.

3
00:00:43,380 --> 00:00:45,960
Now, please give me some good news.

4
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:48,020
Have you agreed to my last proposal?

5
00:00:48,020 --> 00:00:49,060
I have, indeed.

6
00:00:49,060 --> 00:00:53,840
And I wish to sign the agreement pending one small change to be made to the contract.

7
00:00:53,840 --> 00:01:00,300
Margaret, we have been through this for almost a year now, back and forth, making alterations.

8
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:04,580
Are you sure you want to make a sponsorship deal for your clients, or not?

9
00:01:04,580 --> 00:01:09,380
I ask this, because, frankly, some people at my end are running out of patience.

10
00:01:09,380 --> 00:01:10,920
I understand your concerns.

11
00:01:10,920 --> 00:01:17,920
But as I’m sure you understand, we hold our clients’ best interests to be of the utmost concern.

12
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:26,440
We therefore come through the fine details of all contracts, rest assured, we all appreciate your firm’s patience.

13
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:27,520
OK, fine.

14
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:30,600
So what changes do you wish to make?

15
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:34,560
Essentially, we would like the new deal to exclude the Middle East.

16
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:35,460
That’s all.

17
00:01:35,460 --> 00:01:36,620
The Middle East.

18
00:01:36,620 --> 00:01:37,720
Why?

19
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:43,120
My client has a couple of other prospective marketing deals from companies in the Middle East.

20
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:50,020
Those offers, should they materialize, would exclusively employ my client’s image in the Middle East only.

21
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:59,100
Therefore, in order to avoid any conflict, we would need to ensure that both marketing campaigns do not overlap geographically.

22
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:03,240
What business sector in the Middle East are we talking about here?

23
00:02:03,240 --> 00:02:04,560
Real estate.

24
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:07,080
Well, that should be okay then.

25
00:02:07,080 --> 00:02:13,720
So long as the product is very different from our food and beverage market, there should be no conflict of interest.

26
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:17,360
Nevertheless, I will have to run this through my people.

27
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:20,080
I don’t foresee any problem, though.

28
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:36,792
The Middle East is a negligible market for us, but I still need to check this with a couple of departments.

29
00:02:36,792 --> 00:02:50,320
Q1. What does the woman say she will do?

30
00:02:50,320 --> 00:03:08,460
Q2. What does the man say about some people he represents?

31
00:03:08,460 --> 00:03:27,840
Q3. What reason does the woman give for the new deal to exclude the Middle East?

32
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:46,160
Q4. What does the man say about the Middle East?

33
00:03:46,160 --> 00:03:49,820
CONVERSATION 2
Next, we have a special science-related news story.

34
00:03:49,820 --> 00:03:53,920
Paula Hancock is at the Denver Observatory.

35
00:03:53,920 --> 00:03:57,620
Paula, what is the big story over there?

36
00:03:57,620 --> 00:03:58,740
Hi, John.

37
00:03:58,740 --> 00:04:03,040
Yes, all the astronomers on site here are very excited.

38
00:04:03,040 --> 00:04:14,080
In fact, space enthusiasts all across North America and the rest of the Northern Hemisphere will be congregating on mountain tops tonight to watch the night sky.

39
00:04:14,080 --> 00:04:15,280
Why?

40
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:16,540
What’s the big event?

41
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:18,060
Is there an eclipse happening soon?

42
00:04:18,060 --> 00:04:23,940
Tonight, the Earth will come into close proximity with the Oppenheimer comet.

43
00:04:23,940 --> 00:04:29,440
It is the closest our planet has been to such a phenomenon in over 100 years.

44
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:38,040
For this reason, it is expected that thousands of people will gaze up at the sky tonight in order to see this formidable object.

45
00:04:38,040 --> 00:04:40,980
How far away is this comet?

46
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:43,000
Will people be able to see it with the naked eye?

47
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:57,800
The Oppenheimer comet will still be millions of miles away, on the edge of our galaxy, but nevertheless, this is a relatively close distance, close enough for people to observe in good detail through a telescope.

48
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:00,740
People will only see a blur without one.

49
00:05:00,740 --> 00:05:04,760
However, that does not mean one needs professional equipment.

50
00:05:04,760 --> 00:05:12,060
Even the most ordinary of telescopes should be conducive for people to observe and wonder at this flying object.

51
00:05:12,060 --> 00:05:19,220
Many of our viewers will be wondering how they too can take part in this once-in-a-lifetime event.

52
00:05:19,220 --> 00:05:21,640
Where will this comet be in the sky?

53
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:23,660
How can people find it?

54
00:05:23,660 --> 00:05:29,780
The comet will be almost exactly due north, at 60 degrees above the equator.

55
00:05:29,780 --> 00:05:38,300
However, finding the comet is indeed very tricky, and scientists here have told me there are plenty of phone apps that will facilitate this.

56
00:05:38,300 --> 00:05:40,600
How fantastic.

57
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:49,300
Thank you, Paula, for the information.

58
00:05:49,300 --> 00:06:08,980
Q5. What does the woman say about all the astronomers at the Denver Observatory?

59
00:06:08,980 --> 00:06:27,680
Q6. What do we learn from the conversation about the Oppenheimer comet?

60
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:46,940
Q7. What does the woman say people will only see in the sky without a telescope?

61
00:06:46,940 --> 00:07:39,520
Q8. What do scientists at the Denver Observatory advise amateurs to do to facilitate their observation?

