00:18:40,120 --> 00:18:41,780
RECORDING 2
The genetic code of all 1.

137
00:18:41,780 --> 00:18:52,320
5 million known species of animals and plants living on earth will be mapped to help save species from extinction and boost human health.

138
00:18:52,320 --> 00:19:07,840
Scientists hope that cracking the genetic code of plants and animals could help uncover new treatments for infectious diseases, slow aging, improve crops in agriculture and create new bio-materials.

139
00:19:07,840 --> 00:19:23,240
In Britain, organizations including the Natural History Museum, the Royal Botanic Gardens at Kew and the Wellcome Sanger Institute have joined forces to sequence Britain’s 66,000 species of animals and plants.

140
00:19:23,240 --> 00:19:30,120
Dubbed the Darwin Tree of Life Project, it is expected to take 10 years and cost 100 million pounds.

141
00:19:30,120 --> 00:19:35,260
Once completed, all the information will be publicly available to researchers.

142
00:19:35,260 --> 00:19:52,640
Many scientists believe that earth has now entered the sixth mass extinction, with humans creating a toxic mix of habitat loss, pollution and climate change, which has already led to the loss of at least 77 species of mammals and 140 types of birds since 1500.

143
00:19:52,640 --> 00:20:00,680
It is the biggest loss of species since the dinosaurs were wiped out 66 million years ago.

144
00:20:00,680 --> 00:20:13,180
Scientists say that sequencing every species will revolutionize the understanding of biology and evolution, bolster efforts to conserve as well as protect and restore biodiversity.

145
00:20:13,180 --> 00:20:29,100
Dr. Tim Littlewood, head of Life Sciences Department at the Natural History Museum, said whether you are interested in food or disease, the history of how every organism on the planet has adapted to its environment is recorded in its genetic makeup.

146
00:20:29,100 --> 00:20:34,240
How you then harness that is dependent on your ability to understand it.

147
00:20:34,240 --> 00:20:40,300
We will be using modern methods to get a really good window on the present and the past.

148
00:20:40,300 --> 00:20:45,060
And of course, a window on the past gives you a prospective model on the future.

149
00:20:45,060 --> 00:20:59,300
Sir Jim Smith, Director of Science at Wellcome, said, “Try as I might, I can’t think of a more exciting, more relevant, more timely or more internationally inspirational project.

150
00:20:59,300 --> 00:21:05,300
Since 1970, humanity has wiped out 60% of animal populations.

151
00:21:05,300 --> 00:21:10,560
About 23,000 of 80,000 species surveyed are approaching extinction.

152
00:21:10,560 --> 00:21:23,560
We are in the midst of the sixth great extinction event of life on our planet, which not only threatens wildlife species, but also imperils the global food supply.

153
00:21:23,560 --> 00:21:30,100
As scientists, we all realize we desperately need to catalog life on our fragile planet now.

154
00:21:30,100 --> 00:21:36,580
I think we’re making history.

155
00:21:36,580 --> 00:21:40,900
”

156
00:21:40,900 --> 00:22:00,540
Q19. What do scientists hope to do by cracking the genetic code of plants and animals?

157
00:22:00,540 --> 00:22:18,860
Q20. What do many scientists believe with regard to earth?

158
00:22:18,860 --> 00:22:41,540
Q21. How does Sir Jim Smith, Director of Science at Welcome, describe the Darwin Tree of Life Project?

159
00:22:41,540 --> 00:22:52,980
RECORDING 3
John Donne, the English poet, wrote in the 17th century: “No man is an island, entire of itself; every man is a piece of the continent, a part of the main.

160
00:22:52,980 --> 00:23:06,300
” Now, a British academic has claimed that human individuality is indeed just an illusion, because societies are far more interconnected at a mental, physical and cultural level than people realise.

161
00:23:06,300 --> 00:23:23,460
In his new book, The Self Delusion, Professor Tom Oliver, a researcher in the Ecology and Evolution group at the University of Reading, argues there is no such thing as “self” and not even our bodies are truly “us”.

162
00:23:23,460 --> 00:23:40,300
Just as Copernicus realised the Earth is not the center of the universe, Professor Oliver said society urgently needs a Copernican-like revolution to understand people are not detached beings but rather part of one connected identity.

163
00:23:40,300 --> 00:23:52,480
“A significant milestone in the cultural evolution of human minds was the acceptance that the Earth is not the center of the universe, the so-called Copernican Revolution,” he writes.

164
00:23:52,480 --> 00:24:01,640
“However, we have one more big myth to dispose of: that we exist as independent selves at the center of a subjective universe.

165
00:24:01,640 --> 00:24:16,480
You may feel as if you are an independent individual, acting autonomously in the world; that you have unchanging inner self that persists throughout your lifetime, acting as a central anchor-point with the world changing around you.

166
00:24:16,480 --> 00:24:19,180
This is the illusion I seek to tackle.

167
00:24:19,180 --> 00:24:23,140
We are intimately connected to the world around us.

168
00:24:23,140 --> 00:24:36,020
” Professor Oliver argues there are around 37 trillion cells in the body but most have a lifespan of just a few days or weeks, so the material “us” is constantly changing.

169
00:24:36,020 --> 00:24:42,080
In fact, there is no part of your body that has existed for more than 10 years.

170
00:24:42,080 --> 00:24:53,240
“Since our bodies are essentially made anew every few weeks, the material in them alone is clearly insufficient to explain the persistent thread of an identity.

171
00:24:53,240 --> 00:25:06,060
” Professor Oliver claims that individualism is actually bad for society, and only by realising we are a part of a bigger entity can we solve pressing environmental and societal problems.

172
00:25:06,060 --> 00:25:14,900
Through selfish over-consumption we are destroying the natural world and using non-renewable resources at an accelerating rate.

173
00:25:14,900 --> 00:25:25,280
“We are at a critical crossroads as a species where we must rapidly reform our mindsets and behavior to act in less selfish ways,” he said.

174
00:25:25,280 --> 00:25:37,508
So let’s open our eyes to the hidden connections all around us.

175
00:25:37,508 --> 00:25:56,320
Q22. What is indeed just an illusion according to Professor Tom Oliver?

176
00:25:56,320 --> 00:26:18,440
Q23. What does Professor Tom Oliver think of the idea that we exist as independent selves at the center of a subjective universe?

177
00:26:18,440 --> 00:26:38,380
Q24. Why does Professor Tom Oliver claim that the material “us” is constantly changing?

178
00:26:38,380 --> 00:27:01,124
Q25. How can we solve pressing environmental and societal problems according to Professor Tom Oliver?
